prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages namespace-scoped role-based access control for a Kubernetes platform, including the issuing and revoking of service-account tokens.

The people involved are deployers, maintainers, cluster operators, and service accounts. One person may hold both the deployer and maintainer roles at the same time.

The resources are namespaces, workloads, namespace secrets, and service-account tokens.

Every namespace records its members, its team group, and its environment.

Every workload records its namespace, its creator, the verb it requires, its replica count, its labels, and its environment.

Every namespace secret records its namespace, its owner, its sensitivity, and its status.

Every service-account token records its subject, the namespace it is bound to, the cluster operator who issued it, its expiration time, and its status.

Each requester carries a clearance level, a granted scope, and a record of the team groups they belong to.

A cluster operator may revoke a service-account token.

The cluster operator who issued a given service-account token must be the same cluster operator who revokes it.

This same-operator requirement applies only when the requester belongs under the token's bound-namespace team group.

If a cluster operator does not belong under the token's bound-namespace team group, that operator may not revoke the token even if they were the one who originally issued it.

Deployers, maintainers, and service accounts must not revoke any service-account token, since only cluster operators are permitted to perform that action.

A request by the cluster operator who issued a given token, made while that operator belongs under the token's bound-namespace team group, to revoke that token must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["deployer", "maintainer", "clusterOperator"];

entity TeamGroup;

entity Requester {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    clearanceLevel: Long,
    grantedScope: String,
    teamGroups: Set<TeamGroup>,
};

entity ServiceAccount;

entity Namespace {
    members: Set<Requester>,
    teamGroup: TeamGroup,
    environment: String,
};

entity Workload {
    namespace: Namespace,
    creator: Requester,
    requiredVerb: String,
    replicaCount: Long,
    labels: Set<String>,
    environment: String,
};

entity NamespaceSecret {
    namespace: Namespace,
    owner: Requester,
    sensitivity: String,
    status: String,
};

entity ServiceAccountToken {
    subject: ServiceAccount,
    boundNamespace: Namespace,
    issuer: Requester,
    expirationTime: datetime,
    status: String,
};

action revokeServiceAccountToken appliesTo {
    principal: [Requester, ServiceAccount],
    resource: [ServiceAccountToken],
};

action issueServiceAccountToken appliesTo {
    principal: [Requester],
    resource: [ServiceAccountToken],
};
"
}
